    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10], target = 8  ->  [3, 4]\nnums = [5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10], target = 6  ->  [-1, -1]\nnums = [], target = 0                   ->  [-1, -1]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: boundaries"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Plain binary search stops at the first match it meets, which could be anywhere inside a run of equal values. **Boundary** problems ask where something starts or stops — the first and last copy of a value, the edge between two halves that behave differently, the top of a slope. The trick each time is a yes/no question that flips exactly once at the boundary, searched with the lower-bound template."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Here, the first copy of the target is the **lower bound** of `target`. The last copy sits just before the first value **bigger** than the target — and for integers, that's the lower bound of `target + 1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Linear scan from both ends"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function searchRange(nums, target) {\n  let first = 0;\n  while (first < nums.length && nums[first] !== target) first++;\n  if (first === nums.length) return [-1, -1];   // not there\n  let last = nums.length - 1;\n  while (nums[last] !== target) last--;\n  return [first, last];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def search_range(nums, target):\n    if target not in nums:\n        return [-1, -1]\n    first = nums.index(target)\n    last = len(nums) - 1 - nums[::-1].index(target)   # the first match from the right\n    return [first, last]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int first = 0;\n    while (first < nums.length && nums[first] != target) first++;\n    if (first == nums.length) return new int[]{-1, -1};   // not there\n    int last = nums.length - 1;\n    while (nums[last] != target) last--;\n    return new int[]{first, last};\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> searchRange(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    int first = 0;\n    while (first < (int)nums.size() && nums[first] != target) first++;\n    if (first == (int)nums.size()) return {-1, -1};   // not there\n    int last = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (nums[last] != target) last--;\n    return {first, last};\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** A common half-way attempt — binary search for any copy, then walk outwards — is also O(n) when the whole array is the target."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Two lower bounds"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "first-last-position",
      "caption": "Watch two lower-bound searches: one for the first copy of the target, one for the first value past it."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function searchRange(nums, target) {\n  const lowerBound = (t) => {               // the first index with nums[i] >= t\n    let low = 0, high = nums.length;\n    while (low < high) {\n      const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n      if (nums[mid] < t) low = mid + 1;\n      else high = mid;\n    }\n    return low;\n  };\n  const first = lowerBound(target);\n  if (first === nums.length || nums[first] !== target) return [-1, -1];   // not there\n  const last = lowerBound(target + 1) - 1;  // just before the first value bigger than target\n  return [first, last];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def search_range(nums, target):\n    def lower_bound(t):                   # the first index with nums[i] >= t\n        low, high = 0, len(nums)\n        while low < high:\n            mid = (low + high) // 2\n            if nums[mid] < t:\n                low = mid + 1\n            else:\n                high = mid\n        return low\n\n    first = lower_bound(target)\n    if first == len(nums) or nums[first] != target:\n        return [-1, -1]                   # not there\n    return [first, lower_bound(target + 1) - 1]   # just before the first value bigger than target"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] searchRange(int[] nums, int target) {\n    int first = lowerBound(nums, target);\n    if (first == nums.length || nums[first] != target) return new int[]{-1, -1};   // not there\n    int last = lowerBound(nums, target + 1) - 1;   // just before the first value bigger than target\n    return new int[]{first, last};\n}\n\nint lowerBound(int[] nums, int t) {       // the first index with nums[i] >= t\n    int low = 0, high = nums.length;\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] < t) low = mid + 1;\n        else high = mid;\n    }\n    return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> searchRange(vector<int>& nums, int target) {\n    auto first = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);\n    if (first == nums.end() || *first != target) return {-1, -1};   // not there\n    auto past = upper_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);      // the first value bigger than target\n    return {(int)(first - nums.begin()), (int)(past - nums.begin()) - 1};\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log n), space O(1).** Two searches of O(log n) each. If `target + 1` could overflow, use an upper bound (`nums[mid] <= t`) for the second search instead, as the C++ version does."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Linear scan from both ends",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "It may walk **the whole array** when every value is the target.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Two indexes."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Two lower bounds",
          "time": "O(log n)",
          "timeWhy": "**Two binary searches** of O(log n) each.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only the bounds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Empty array** — `[-1, -1]`.",
        "**Target missing** — between values, below all, above all.",
        "**The whole array is the target** — `[8, 8, 8]` gives `[0, 2]`.",
        "**One copy** — first and last are the same index."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Count occurrences in a sorted array** — `upper − lower` from the same two searches.",
        "**Search Insert Position** — the first search alone (Pattern 1).",
        "**Majority Element in a sorted array** — check whether the first copy of `nums[n/2]` plus n/2 still holds the same value."
      ]
    }
